DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     pprrooccssttaatt utility displays detailed information about the processes iden‐
     tified by the _p_i_d arguments, or if the --aa flag is used, all processes.
     It can also display information extracted from a process core file, if
     the core file is specified as the argument.

     The ppaarrggss, ppeennvv and ppwwddxx utilities display the arguments, environment,
     and current working directory, respectively of the process specified by
     _p_i_d argument.  They mimic the behavior of Solaris utilities of the same
     names.

     If the ----lliibbxxoo flag is specified the output is generated via libxo(3) in
     a selection of different human and machine readable formats.  See
     xo_parse_args(3) for details on command line arguments.

     The following commands are available for pprrooccssttaatt:

     _a_d_v_l_o_c_k
             Print information about advisory locks on files.  All three types
             of locks are listed, BSD-style lockf(2), POSIX-style fcntl(2)
             _F___S_E_T_L_K, and remote lockd(8) locks used by NFSv3.

             Note that neither the --aa option nor _p_i_d list can be used to limit
             the display of the locks, mostly because some types of locks do
             not have local (or any) owning processes.

     _a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t_(_s_) | --cc
             Display command line arguments for the process.

             Substring commands are accepted.

     _a_u_x_v | --xx
             Display ELF auxiliary vector for the process.

     _b_a_s_i_c   Print basic process statistics (this is the default).

     _b_i_n_a_r_y | --bb
             Display binary information for the process.

             Substring commands are accepted.

     _c_r_e_d_e_n_t_i_a_l_(_s_) | --ss
             Display security credential information for the process.

             Substring commands are accepted.

     _c_p_u_s_e_t | _c_s | --SS
             Display the cpuset information for the thread.

     _e_n_v_i_r_o_n_m_e_n_t | --ee
             Display environment variables for the process.

             Substring commands are accepted.

     _f_i_l_e_(_s_) | _f_d_(_s_) | --ff
             Display file descriptor information for the process.

             If the --CC subcommand flag is used then additional capability in‐
             formation is printed.

     _k_q_u_e_u_e_(_s_) [--vv]
             Display the events registered in the process kqueues.  The filter
             name, filter-specific identifier, flags, filter-specific flags,
             system and user data, and event status are displayed.  If the --vv
             verbose flag is provided to the subcommand, the values of the ext
             array are displayed as well.

             For flags, a string is printed consisting of the following sym‐
             bols corresponding to set flags:
             O  _E_V___O_N_E_S_H_O_T
             C  _E_V___C_L_E_A_R
             R  _E_V___R_E_C_E_I_P_T
             D  _E_V___D_I_S_P_A_T_C_H
             d  _E_V___D_R_O_P
             1  _E_V___F_L_A_G_1
             2  _E_V___F_L_A_G_2

             For status:
             A  _K_N_O_T_E___S_T_A_T_U_S___A_C_T_I_V_E
             Q  _K_N_O_T_E___S_T_A_T_U_S___Q_U_E_U_E_D
             D  _K_N_O_T_E___S_T_A_T_U_S___D_I_S_A_B_L_E_D
             d  _K_N_O_T_E___S_T_A_T_U_S___D_E_T_A_C_H_E_D
             K  _K_N_O_T_E___S_T_A_T_U_S___K_Q_U_E_U_E

     _k_s_t_a_c_k | --kk
             Display the stacks of kernel threads in the process, excluding
             stacks of threads currently running on a CPU and threads with
             stacks swapped to disk.

             If the --vv subcommand option is used (or the command flag is re‐
             peated), function offsets as well as function names are printed.

     _p_a_r_g_s   Display arguments for the process.

     _p_e_n_v    Display environment variables for the process.

     _p_t_l_w_p_i_n_f_o | --LL
             Display LWP info for the process pertaining to its signal driven
             exit.

     _p_w_d_x    Display current working directory for the process.

     _r_l_i_m_i_t | --ll
             Display resource limits for the process.

     _r_l_i_m_i_t_u_s_a_g_e
             Display the usage of the resource limits for the process.

     _r_u_s_a_g_e | --rr
             Display resource usage information for the process.

             If the --vv (or --HH) subcommand flag is used then per-thread statis‐
             tics are printed, rather than per-process statistics.  The second
             field in the table will list the thread ID to which the row of
             information corresponds.

     _s_i_g_n_a_l_(_s_) | --ii
             Display signal pending and disposition information for the
             process.

             If the --nn subcommand option is used, the signal numbers are shown
             instead of signal names.

             Substring commands are accepted.

     _t_h_r_e_a_d_(_s_) | --tt
             Display thread information for the process.

     _t_s_i_g_n_a_l_(_s_) | --jj
             Display signal pending and blocked information for the process's
             threads.

             If the --nn subcommand option is used, the signal numbers are shown
             instead of signal names.

             Substring commands are accepted.

     _v_m | --vv
             Display virtual memory mappings for the process.

     All options generate output in the format of a table, the first field of
     which is the process ID to which the row of information corresponds.  The
     --hh flag may be used to suppress table headers.

     The --ww flag may be used to specify a wait interval at which to repeat the
     printing of the requested process information.  If the --ww flag is not
     specified, the output will not repeat.

     Information for VM, file descriptor, and cpuset options is available only
     to the owner of a process or the superuser.  A cpuset value displayed as
     -1 means that the information is either invalid or not available.

